# Linux Package Management: Updating, Removing & Troubleshooting

1. Updating Packages
Before installing or upgrading software, update the package repository information:

sudo apt update
This does not upgrade your packages. It downloads the latest package information from configured repositories.

Upgrade installed packages
sudo apt upgrade

Update and upgrade together
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade

Full upgrade
sudo apt full-upgrade
full-upgrade can install or remove packages if necessary to complete the upgrade.

Check which packages can be upgraded
apt list --upgradable

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Removing Packages
Remove a package
sudo apt remove package_name

Example:
sudo apt remove nmap
This removes the software but generally keeps its configuration files.

Completely remove a package
sudo apt purge package_name

Example:
sudo apt purge nmap
This removes the package and its system configuration files.

Remove unused dependencies
sudo apt autoremove
Useful after removing software whose dependencies are no longer required.

Clean downloaded package files
sudo apt clean
Removes downloaded .deb files from the local package cache.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. Troubleshooting Package Problems
Problem: Broken dependencies

If you get dependency errors, try:

sudo apt --fix-broken install

Then:

sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade
